                        "username": "aditya-K2"
                    },
                    "message": "@molberding@nvidia.com Can you please clarify what the issue was? it will help in understanding the patch more"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "updated."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Does https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/82594/7 make this any better?\n\nAlso need @haghera@nvidia.com and @akurdunkar@nvidia.com to review this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 14,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "it may prevent some level of the problems, but it doesn't fix the real problem. Which is that we are binding whatever the last thing we got in to the next packet we're getting, and the buffer's are client buffers. So no matter what the next message is, we're writing it into the last buffer we got. This led to all sorts of problems as sends and send completes and receives can all interleave. This patchset will change to just keep a buffer internally."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "tested?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 25,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "ah yeah, I missed adding that. will update"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Why doesn't this use the GetIid() introduced in an older patchset?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 42,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "Not sure if you're talking about 82594 or the patch earlier in this patch series. If the former, I just didn't know about it, if the latter that function just retrieves the next iid."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 60,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm confused, doesn't this mean this app now responds to this EID?  Doesn't that mean that we're now binding to the eid of the GPU?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 60,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "it means that all packets with `msgType` on this eid will get received by this socket. per discussions on the side, I'm fine with keeping this as a single socket design and changing the buffering structure."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 215,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we make error log include `eid`?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 215,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "yep."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 219,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Are we running into object lifetime issue if we don't move out `reqCtx` first before calling callaback?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp",
                    "line": 219,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "no, this is not a strictly necessary change. We're not multithreaded. This came along for the ride when I was trying to debug, I'm fine reverting it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.hpp",
                    "line": 108,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Does this mean that we now have a separate socket opened for each eid? would this have a performance impact? To avoid it can `Requester` class maintain state based on `eid` similar to `QueuingRequester` class? Or we can just combine the two class into one?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.hpp",
                    "line": 108,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Marc Olberding",
                        "email": "molberding@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "molbear"
                    },
                    "message": "we either need to change to one socket per device or change the buffering structure, which after chatting with ed is the direction we'll likely be heading. Requester had previously maintained state based on eid, but it was broken, for reasons explained in the commit. We'll be moving to a structure where the socket keeps a buffer instead of binding."
                }
            ],
